<<<
5. Accent & pronunciation in p.p. (or participial adjective or noun) & verb. Beside many of the verbs formed from Latin supine stems (animate, dilute, extract, &c.) there are passive participles of the same spelling, now used as adjectives or nouns.
===
**5. Accent & pronunciation in p.p. (or participial adjective or noun) & verb.** Beside many of the verbs formed from Latin supine stems (*animate, dilute, extract, &c.*) there are passive participles of the same spelling, now used as adjectives or nouns.
>>>

<<<
co’nsummate v., Consu’mmate a.
===
co’nsummate v., consu’mmate a.
>>>

<<<
dilu’te v., dilute a. ; extra’ct
===
dilu’te v., di’lute a. ; extra’ct
>>>

<<<
(B) the obscuring of the vowel of -ate, as in advocate v., advocate n.; animate v., animate a.; articulate v., articulate a. ; compassionate v., compassionate a.;
===
(B) the obscuring of the vowel of *-ate*, as in advocāte v., advocate n.; animāte v., animate a.; articulāte v., articulate a. ; compassionāte v., compassionate a.;
>>>

<<<
degenerate v., degenerate a.; delegate v., delegate n.; deliberate v., deliberate a.; designate v., designate a. ; desolate v., desolate a.; elaborate v., elaborate a.;
===
degenerāte v., degenerate a.; delegāte v., delegate n.; deliberāte v., deliberate a.; designāte v., designate a. ; desolāte v., desolate a.; elaborāte v., elaborate a.;
>>>

<<<
estimate v., estimate n.; legitimate v., legitimate a.; moderate v., moderate a.; regenerate v., regenerate a.; reprobate v., reprobate n. ; separate V., separate a.; subordinate v., subordinate a. & n. ;
===
estimāte v., estimate n.; legitimāte v., legitimate a.; moderāte v., moderate a.; regenerāte v., regenerate a.; reprobāte v., reprobate n. ; separāte v., separate a.; subordināte v., subordinate a. & n. ;
>>>

<<<
or (C) a change of consonant sound, as in diffuse {-z) v., diffuse (-s) a. ; refuse (-z) v., refuse (-s) n.
===
or (C) a change of consonant sound, as in diffuse (-z) v., diffuse (-s) a. ; refuse (-z) v., refuse (-s) n.
>>>

<<<
Words are referred to this article {PARTICIPLES 5 A or B or C) to draw

PASSABLE | attention to a double pronunciation 3; see also NOUN & VERB ACCENT.
===
Words are referred to this article (^^PARTICIPLES^^ 5 A or B or C) to draw

+ attention to a double pronunciation ; see also ^^NOUN & VERB ACCENT^^.
>>>

<<<
parti-coloured, party-, partic-. The first is recommended.
===
**parti-coloured, party-, partic-.** The first is recommended.
>>>

<<<
particularize makes

-zable; see MUTE £.
===
**particularize** makes *-zable*; see ^^MUTE E^^.
>>>

<<<
parting. The British Empire is at the p. of the ways.
===
**parting.** *The British Empire is at the p. of the ways.*
>>>

<<<
partisan, -zan. Whether
===
**partisan, -zan.** Whether
>>>

<<<
be well to make partisa’n (or -za’n) invariable
===
be well to make *partisa’n* (or *-za’n*) invariable
>>>

<<<
the weapon: partisa’n adherent; par’tizan weapon.
===
the weapon: *partisa’n* adherent; *par’tizan* weapon.
>>>

<<<
partitive. See TECHNICAL TERMS.
===
**partitive.** See ^^TECHNICAL TERMS^^.
>>>

<<<
party. For this in the sense person, see POPULARIZED TECHNICALITIES.
===
**party.** For this in the sense *person*, see ^^POPULARIZED TECHNICALITIES^^.
>>>

<<<
parvenu, pas. See FRENCH WORDS.
===
**parvenu, pas.** See ^^FRENCH WORDS^^.
>>>

<<<
pasha, pacha. Spell pasha & pronounce pah’sha.
===
**pasha, pacha.** Spell *pasha* & pronounce pah’sh*a*.
>>>

<<<
pasquinade.

See LAMPOON for synonyms.
===
**pasquinade.** See ^^LAMPOON^^ for synonyms.
>>>

<<<
pass makes passed for its past tense (You passed me by), & for its p.p. used verbally (It has passed out of use); but when the p.p. has become a mere adjective it is spelt past (In past times).
===
**pass** makes *passed* for its past tense (*You passed me by*), & for its p.p. used verbally (*It has passed out of use*); but when the p.p. has become a mere adjective it is spelt *past* (*In past times*).
>>>

<<<
in Those times have passed away (p.p.), Those times are passed away (INTRANSITIVE P.P.), Those times are past (adjective).
===
in *Those times have passed away* (p.p.), *Those times are passed away* (^^INTRANSITIVE P.P.^^), *Those times are past* (adjective).
>>>

